finally after trying to implement the functionality of the icon change according to the modifier, the project has exploded, i wake up with the bright idea that instead of having all the leds always off and turn them on when pressed, as takuya does, i should create a lighting pattern where a random number of keys lights between true/false states in the style of classic mainframes, which would faithfully replicate the alien1 computational aesthetic that i want to follow (and a lighting effect that i have never seen on any keyboard), also it would not be difficult to make the key to light up and fade away on press, good news are that these patterns are implemented without problems and the overall result is already 95% similar to the complete project that i aspired to

the bad news is that gemini cannot stop corrupting the icon drawing, and at certain flashing, the led lighting began to emit random colors at the base layer, no amount of vibe bugfixing was able to get out of this dead end, tried everything: eprom nuke, clean base firmware, custom firmware, nothing works, gemini began to desperately look for solutions anywhere and started to blame the buses and the architecture of the qmk compiler, which i don't doubt they have some real residue, but it was plain impossible that was the problem, so after a couple of chocolate breaks, i realize that the default firmware emits different colors on each half (impossible), a little more checking shows that there is an underglow led on the master side that broke again (possibly burnt due to the overvoltage of previous bugfixings), technical stop to resolder leds and problem solved, but with the endless problems of drawing in the oled, and key lighting conflicts with the vial driver, gemini agrees with me (surprise) to delete vial and move to via, so i did, but as the whole project had been done for vial so far, i decide to go slowly by surely (and a lot of work already done), and port with the utmost care: first thing i do is to create a repository for my own mental sanity since with so much vibe i was not able to go back to the exact version of the source code that worked before going downhill, which would have identified the hardware problem of the faulty led immediately after flashing an already tested firmware
